# Who owns which policy layer.
#
# The three-layer Rego bundle exists so that three DIFFERENT groups can own three
# different questions without editing each other's files:
#
#   platform.rego  — the platform team: global guardrails, and the single decision
#                    the gateway queries. Domain teams must not edit it.
#   domain.rego    — each domain team, for the MCP server(s) they run: which
#                    backends are enabled and which app roles may reach them.
#   tool.rego      — the owning tool team: per-tool entitlements and argument
#                    constraints.
#
# All three files say so in their headers and name CODEOWNERS as the mechanism.
# This file is that mechanism. In this reference repo every line points at the
# same person, because there is only one — in a real deployment the whole value
# is that these lines point at three different teams, and a change to a tool's
# argument cap cannot be merged by the person who wrote the platform guardrails.
# Splitting them is a find-and-replace, not a redesign.

/policy/bundle/mcp/platform.rego            @Jayjo86
/policy/bundle/mcp/domain.rego              @Jayjo86
/policy/bundle/mcp/tool.rego                @Jayjo86

# The facts all three layers decide against, and the gateway-side half of the
# same table (server, required_role, regulatory tags, least-privilege scope).
# They are two hand-maintained copies of one fact — tests/test_registry_policy_
# agreement.py fails if they disagree — so they change together, under one owner.
/policy/data.json                           @Jayjo86
/gateway-fastmcp/app/tools/registry.py      @Jayjo86

# The controls the policy layers rest on: inbound OAuth, the PEP, and the
# no-passthrough downstream exchange.
/gateway-fastmcp/app/auth.py                @Jayjo86
/gateway-fastmcp/app/middleware/            @Jayjo86
/gateway-fastmcp/app/downstream/            @Jayjo86
